  "text": "Most people click away from videos like this because. Well, honestly, I probably would have to. So today I just wanted to talk about something I noticed. I mean, it's not really complicated, so. Yeah, but something interesting happened. Last week. We tested the exact same landing page with one small change. The first version converted at 2.8%. The second converted at 5.1%. We didn't change the product, we didn't change the pricing. We only changed where people looked first. And that's when everything started making sense. Imagine taking a walk into a store where the entrance points you towards the checkout instead of the shelves. That's basically what happened. So what we're really optimizing isn't attention, it's decision making. I was gonna. I was gonna explain this with marketing jargon, actually. Forget that. Think of attention like water flowing downhill. If you redirect the flow, you redirect the outcome. Because people don't buy when they understand, they buy when they believe. So just let that sink in. The surprising part is that almost nobody measures this directly, which makes comparing videos a little messy. I haven't found one transcript where the software thought I said Hyperplane. Honestly, I think it wrote Hyperplane instead. If you want to see the full breakdown, there's a link in the description. It includes the complete experiment on every screenshot we use. Thanks for watching.",
